>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does central heating installation cost on the Wirral?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A full new system starts from £4,495 for a 2-bed home, £5,295 for a 3-bed and £6,295 for a 4-bed, including VAT. A combi conversion starts from £2,890.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Home</th><th>Typical radiators</th><th>Price, inc VAT</th><th>Typical time</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>2-bed flat or house</strong></td><td>Up to 6</td><td>from £4,495</td><td>2 to 3 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>3-bed terrace or semi</strong></td><td>7 to 10</td><td>from £5,295</td><td>3 to 5 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>4-bed detached</strong></td><td>11 or more</td><td>from £6,295</td><td>5 to 7 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Combi conversion</strong></td><td>Keeps your radiators</td><td>from £2,890</td><td>1 to 2 days</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The price depends on the number of radiators, the pipe runs, the walls and floors, and the boiler you choose. That is why we visit first. The survey is free, and the price we give you is fixed and in writing.</p>
      <p>Need just one more radiator? See our <a href="/radiators-wirral">Wirral radiator prices</a>, from £245 supplied and fitted. Only the boiler failing? See <a href="/boiler-installation-wirral">boiler installation on the Wirral</a> from £2,195.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="homes">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which central heating system suits my Wirral home?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It depends on where you live and how old the house is. The Wirral has very mixed housing, so we never fit the same system everywhere.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Birkenhead, Rock Ferry, Wallasey, New Brighton (CH41, CH42, CH44, CH45)</h3>
          <p>Mostly Victorian and Edwardian terraces. A combi often suits them. But some have low mains pressure, so we test it first. If it is low, a system boiler with a small cylinder works better.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Hoylake, West Kirby, Caldy (CH47, CH48)</h3>
          <p>Lots of period houses. Many were never fully heated, or have cold rooms. Solid walls and tight floor spaces make pipe routes harder. Pressure is often good, so an unvented cylinder can suit homes with more than one bathroom.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sg-cols" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Heswall, Pensby, Bebington, Bromborough, Neston (CH60 to CH64)</h3>
          <p>Bigger detached and semi-detached homes. These usually need a system boiler and a hot water cylinder, not a combi. Upstairs and downstairs often run as separate zones. The survey here takes longer, as we size the boiler, zones and cylinder.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Prenton, Moreton, Upton (CH43, CH46, CH49)</h3>
          <p>Mostly post-war semis with central heating already. Often the system is tired, not broken. A new boiler with a <a href="/power-flushing-wirral">power flush</a> or an extra radiator can fix it for far less than a new system.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3>Do you cover every CH postcode?</h3>
      <p>Yes. The survey and price are the same wherever you live on the peninsula.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Towns and postcodes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Birkenhead and central</strong></td><td>Birkenhead CH41, Rock Ferry CH42, Prenton CH43, Wallasey CH44, New Brighton CH45</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>West and north</strong></td><td>Moreton CH46, Hoylake CH47, West Kirby and Caldy CH48, Upton CH49</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>South Wirral</strong></td><td>Heswall CH60, Pensby CH61, Bromborough CH62, Bebington CH63, Neston CH64</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Signs your home needs new central heating</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>No central heating at all</strong>, common in some older CH41, CH42 and CH47 homes</li>
        <li><strong>Rooms that stay cold</strong> with the heating on</li>
        <li><strong>Cold spots</strong> on radiators that bleeding does not fix</li>
        <li><strong>A slow system</strong> that takes ages to warm up</li>
        <li><strong>A poor EPC rating</strong> of D or lower, which can affect selling or letting</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="pressure">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Should I have a combi or a system boiler on the Wirral?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It depends on your mains water pressure and how many bathrooms you have. We test the pressure at every survey before we recommend a boiler.</p>
      <p>A combi heats water straight from the mains. It needs good pressure and flow to work well. In a low-pressure terrace, the upstairs shower can be weak. Fitting a combi in the wrong home is a costly mistake.</p>
      <p>A system boiler stores hot water in a cylinder. It suits homes with two or more bathrooms used at once. In bigger Heswall and West Kirby homes, an unvented cylinder gives strong showers in every bathroom. See our <a href="/hot-water-cylinders-wirral">Wirral hot water cylinder prices</a>.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Bigger homes need zones.</strong> Under Building Regulations, a home over 150 square metres needs at least two heating zones with their own timing. We plan zones at the survey for larger south Wirral homes.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="included">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What is included in a new central heating system?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Everything you need for a working system. There are no hidden extras.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>New A-rated boiler</strong> from Baxi, Worcester Bosch or Vaillant, sized for your home</li>
        <li><strong>New radiators</strong>, each sized for its room, with TRVs</li>
        <li><strong>All new pipework</strong>, routed to keep it out of sight where we can</li>
        <li><strong>Smart or programmable controls</strong>, with zones for bigger homes</li>
        <li><strong>Magnetic filter</strong> to protect the boiler from sludge</li>
        <li><strong>System clean and inhibitor</strong> to BS 7593</li>
        <li><strong>Balancing</strong>, so every room heats evenly</li>
        <li><strong>Gas safety checks and Benchmark</strong> filled in on the day</li>
        <li><strong>Old boiler, radiators and tanks</strong> taken away</li>
        <li><strong>12-month workmanship guarantee</strong> on all our work</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Want a smart thermostat that needs new wiring? Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ideal Electrical Solutions</a> does the electrical work.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="time">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How long does central heating installation take on the Wirral?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A full system usually takes 3 to 5 days. A large south Wirral home can take 5 to 7 days. Here is how it goes.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Free survey.</strong> We check the layout, walls, floors, mains pressure and boiler position. You get a fixed written price and agreed dates.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 1: strip out.</strong> We cover floors, drain the old system and take it out.</li>
        <li><strong>Days 2 and 3: pipes and radiators.</strong> We run the new pipes and hang the radiators. Floorboards go back as each room is done.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 4: boiler.</strong> Our Gas Safe registered engineers fit the boiler, flue, gas supply and controls.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 5: clean, test, hand over.</strong> We clean the system, add inhibitor, fit the filter and balance each radiator. We show you the controls.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Solid-wall terraces with no space under the floor can add a day or two. We never leave you without a safe system overnight.</p>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Want a fixed price for your Wirral home?</strong> Book a free survey. No pressure to go ahead.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="paperwork">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What paperwork and warranty do I get?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">You get the maker's boiler warranty, our 12-month workmanship guarantee, the Benchmark checklist and a Building Regulations certificate.</p>
      <p>We register the boiler warranty for you and email your documents the same day. The new boiler is notified to Building Control through the Gas Safe Register. Your certificate then comes by post.</p>
      <p>Keep the warranty valid with a yearly <a href="/boiler-service-wirral">boiler service</a>, £95 with us. Landlords need a <a href="/gas-safety-certificate-wirral">gas safety certificate</a> every 12 months, from £120.</p>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Smell gas?</strong> Call the National Gas Emergency line on 0800 111 999 first. Then call us.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

</ServiceGuide>
